#{[17592186377708 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377708 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74965" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377708 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377708 #edb/ref 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377708 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6323a167-9fc1-5848-81f1-63d34a9c63ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377708 #edb/ref 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot 0}{{0}^{3}+{0}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths. \n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=2x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+8{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377709 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377709 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377709 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377709 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759