path $y={x}^{2}$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb1f3bd1-4155-5e86-902f-cd91483f7d31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380062 #edb/ref 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+(x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{({x}^{2})}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380063 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380063 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380063 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380063 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380063 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380063 #edb/ref 1076 #ed