#edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407845 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186407828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407845 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e093674e-caf5-5f8d-97ff-ca3172fa6889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407848 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407848 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407848 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407848 #edb/ref 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407848 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d70f2b2e-153e-52a8-b763-677074637b88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407848 #edb/ref 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}-2}{{2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2}-2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2})^{2}-4}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy-2}{(xy-2)(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} & =\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 1+2)(\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{16}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407850 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407850 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23171e8d-210d-5c2c-9bfd-bb23fda5